1
00:00:00,200 --> 00:00:03,900
Football broadcasts tell you what happened. Nobody tells you why it mattered.

2
00:00:04,100 --> 00:00:08,900
It sees danger coming: Liverpool's chance of a shot in the next 10 seconds climbs to 60%.

3
00:00:09,200 --> 00:00:15,400
Then it freezes the real frame, reads the players and replays the moment 200 times.

4
00:00:15,600 --> 00:00:18,900
Szoboszlai shot. The pass to Salah was worth 2 more points of win chance.

5
00:00:19,200 --> 00:00:24,800
Every line is drawn on the real pitch, through the broadcast camera.

6
00:00:25,000 --> 00:00:29,900
Eze shot. Going back to Saka was the better ball.

7
00:00:30,200 --> 00:00:39,900
And it says when the player got it right: the pass into the box was no better.

8
00:00:40,200 --> 00:00:45,900
Every refereeing call explained from the tracking: the run, the contact, the closing speed.

9
00:00:46,100 --> 00:00:51,400
An assessment, not a ruling: about 15 km/h into the challenge, outside the box.

10
00:00:51,800 --> 00:01:01,100
The same intelligence becomes an analyst's breakdown, a casual fan's line, and a player's story.

11
00:01:01,500 --> 00:01:08,200
Kill the newsroom mid-moment: the run resumes from its checkpoint and the cue still airs.

12
00:01:08,500 --> 00:01:13,700
A producer can veto anything before it airs.

13
00:01:14,100 --> 00:01:22,600
Measured, not claimed: 141 of 141 on-screen numbers grounded, 500 of 500 planted errors caught.

